<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Guy of Lusignan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1150–<a href="/years/1194">1194</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as King Guy</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Guy of Lusignan was a French nobleman who became King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> through his marriage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sibylla-queen-of-jerusalem">Queen Sibylla</a>. He commanded the crusader army that was defeated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hattin">Battle of Hattin</a> in 1187, where he was captured. The defeat allowed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Empire</a> to take Jerusalem and most of the crusader kingdom.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/guy-of-lusignan.jpg" alt="Guy of Lusignan" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Museum of the The Order of St. John and The University of Birmingham · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coin_issued_by_Guy_de_Lusignan_during_when_he_was_Lord_of_Cyprus_(Crop).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The victory was total: the army was destroyed, the relic of the True Cross captured and <b>King Guy</b> of Lusignan taken prisoner.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>At the Battle of Hattin on 4 July 1187, Saladin surrounded and destroyed the army of <b>King Guy</b> of Lusignan, perhaps 20,000 men, cut off from water near Lake Tiberias.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Guy of Lusignan</b>, King of Jerusalem, began the siege of Acre, his first significant counterattack against Saladin and part of what became the Third Crusade.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-28">28 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Near Tiberias in Galilee, Sultan Saladin destroyed the army of the Crusader Kingdom of Jerusalem and captured its king, <b>Guy of Lusignan</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-4">4 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The county was frequently held by members of the royal family, including Amalric and later <b>Guy of Lusignan</b>, and its revenues came from the port and coastal farmland.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-jaffa-and-ascalon">County of Jaffa and Ascalon</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1192 Richard I sold Cyprus, recently taken from Isaac Komnenos and returned by the Templars, to <b>Guy of Lusignan</b>, the deposed king of Jerusalem.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-cyprus">Kingdom of Cyprus</a></li></ul></section>
